  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0002 database is designed to manage various aspects of a system, including user management, data types, menus, roles, settings, permissions, and translations. At its core, the database contains a table for users, which stores information such as a unique identifier, an avatar, a reference to a role, and custom settings. Each user can be associated with one or more roles through the user_roles table, which links a user's ID to a role's ID. Roles themselves are stored in a separate table, where each role has a unique name, display name, and timestamps for creation and update. The roles table is also linked to the permissions table, which stores unique keys and references to the table name they pertain to. The permission_role table then links permissions to roles, allowing for fine-grained control over what actions can be performed by each role. The data_types table stores information about different data types, including their name, slug, display name, and other metadata. The data_rows table links data types to specific fields, where each field has a unique name, type, display name, and permissions settings such as browse, read, edit, add, and delete. The menus and menu_items tables manage the structure of a menu system, where each menu item has a title, URL, target, and other attributes, and can be organized into a hierarchical structure with parent-child relationships. The translations table stores translations for various fields across different tables, including the table name, column name, foreign key, locale, and value. Finally, the settings table stores key-value pairs of system settings, including their display name, value, and details.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nVoyager is an admin panel package for Laravel, developed by The Control Group. It provides a rapid scaffolding solution for backend management, simplifying common administrative tasks within Laravel applications. The package is designed to enhance usability for developers by offering pre-built functionalities for managing app data and user interfaces, thereby reducing development time and effort.\n\n Objectives\n\nThe primary objective of Voyager is to streamline the development of admin interfaces for Laravel applications. It aims to provide:\n- A cohesive admin interface for managing application data.\n- Easily configurable tools for creating and handling BREAD/CRUD operations.\n- Efficient media and menu management functionalities.\n\n Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Primary users who integrate Voyager into their Laravel applications to manage backend admin tasks.\n- Admin Users: Users who access the admin panel to perform data management, media handling, and menu building.\n- The Control Group: Developers and maintainers of the Voyager package.\n\n Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Admin Interface: Allows users to add, edit, and delete app data. \n2. Menu Builder: Facilitates creation and management of site menus, allowing for the addition, editing, and deletion of items across various menu locations such as header, sidebar, and footer.\n3. Media Manager: Provides capabilities for uploading, renaming, and deleting files. Users can also create and organize folders.\n4. CRUD/BREAD Builder: Enables the generation of CRUD operations and BREAD functionalities across application tables. \n5. User Roles and Permissions: Manages user roles and permissions to access different admin functionalities.\n6. Data Type Management: Supports defining and managing different data types and their attributes.\n\n Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: The admin panel should handle operations efficiently to ensure a smooth user experience without unnecessary delays.\n- Scalability: The system should support scaling as the application data and user base grow.\n- Security: All data interactions and file management operations should be secured to prevent unauthorized access and data breaches.\n- Usability: The interface should be intuitive and easy for both developers and admin users to navigate and operate.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0002.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0002\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `avatar` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `settings` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `users_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permissions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `key` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `table_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permission_role` (\n  `permission_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`permission_id`, `role_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_role_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`permission_id`) REFERENCES `permissions` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_role_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `data_types` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `metadata` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `data_rows` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `data_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `field_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `field_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `permissions` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `data_rows_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`data_type_id`) REFERENCES `data_types` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `menus` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `menu_items` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `menu_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `url` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `target` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `parent_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `menu_items_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`menu_id`) REFERENCES `menus` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `menu_items_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`parent_id`) REFERENCES `menu_items` (`id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `translations` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `table_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `column_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `foreign_key` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `locale` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `value` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `settings` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `key` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `value` text NOT NULL,\n  `details` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
